  <!-- COMMON MASONRY PROBLEMS -->
  <section class="lp-section lp-problems" style="background:#F5F3EF;border-top:1px solid #D8D2C8;">
    <div class="lp-section-inner">
      <div class="lp-section-label">Common Masonry Problems in Winnetka</div>
      <h2 class="lp-section-h2">What We Fix — <em>Most Often in Winnetka</em></h2>
      <div class="lp-problems-grid">
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Tudor Revival Decorative Brick Joint Failure</h3>
          <p>Tudor Revival joint failure disrupts both structure and appearance. Repointing requires matching mortar composition, joint profile, and tooling technique to preserve the original decorative character.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Limestone Lintel and Sill Cracking</h3>
          <p>Open limestone joints let water infiltrate, causing stone deterioration and structural damage behind the facade. Limestone repair requires softer, more flexible mortar than adjacent brick.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Lake Moisture-Driven Efflorescence</h3>
          <p>Lake Michigan moisture drives persistent efflorescence — salt deposits showing water moving through the wall. Requires tuckpointing to close entry points, sometimes breathable waterproofing to manage ongoing exposure.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="lp-problem-card">
          <div class="lp-problem-accent"></div>
          <h3>Estate Chimney Crown and Liner Deterioration</h3>
          <p>Lake moisture and wind accelerate crown and liner deterioration on Winnetka estate chimneys, allowing water infiltration into the structure and interior. Repair must address both function and architectural continuity.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

